    "body": "## What does this implement/fix?\n\n| Property | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Method | `hashCode` |\n| Class | `net.gaast.giggity.Schedule$Item` |\n| File | `app/src/main/java/net/gaast/giggity/Schedule.java` |\n| Specification | `MessageDigestSpec` |\n| Analysis tool | `ape` |\n\n## Summary\n\n### Observed dynamic-analysis failure\n\nThe dynamic-analysis run by `ape` reported (`MessageDigestSpec`) while exercising `net.gaast.giggity.Schedule$Item.hashCode`:\n\n> expecting one of {SHA-256, SHA-384, SHA-512} but found MD5.\n\n### Root cause\n\nSchedule.Item.hashCode() invokes java.security.MessageDigest.getInstance(\"MD5\"), and MD5 is outside the verified MessageDigestSpec allow-list.\n\n### Correction strategy\n\nApply the authorized hashCode-override migration: eliminate MessageDigest rather than substituting another governed digest, and use an idiomatic non-cryptographic int hash over the same live data currently represented by getUrl() (Schedule URL and guid-or-id). Preserve the override signature and int result while treating any changed exact integer values as a compatibility item requiring the specified consumer and persistence review.\n\n### Coordinated changes\n\n- `app/src/main/java/net/gaast/giggity/Schedule.java`: ByteBuffer was used only to fold the first four bytes of the retired MD5 digest into the hashCode result.; MessageDigest and NoSuchAlgorithmException were used only by the retired MD5-based hashCode implementation.; Use the idiomatic non-cryptographic hash for the same sole input as before: getUrl(), which combines the enclosing schedule URL and guid-or-id discriminator. This preserves the public override signature and int result while eliminating MessageDigest, its algorithm fallback, and digest-byte folding.\n- `app/src/test/java/net/gaast/giggity/ScheduleHashCodeTest.java`: Adds non-reflective JUnit coverage for the supplied cross-file contract: Schedule.Item instances with equal enclosing Schedule URLs and equal guid-or-id discriminator data must have equal hash codes, and equality must imply hash-code equality. The assertions deliberately do not depend on the prior MD5-derived integer.\n\n### Verification included\n\n- Run the relevant JVM unit-test target containing app/src/test/java/net/gaast/giggity/ScheduleHashCodeTest.java.\n- Run the JVM unit-test target that includes app/src/test/java/net/gaast/giggity/ScheduleHashCodeTest.java.\n- Verify items with equal Schedule URLs and equal guid-or-id discriminator values return equal hashCode values.\n- Verify Schedule.Item equality implies equal hashCode values.\n\n### Residual review requirements\n\nThe patch remains review-only until these assumptions are confirmed:\n\n- The reviewed scope classifies this value as an internal runtime discriminator; no persisted, transmitted, exported, cross-release, or external exact-value consumer was identified.\n- The existing super.hashCode() fallback demonstrates that callers already tolerate non-digest-derived and process-unstable values.\n- getUrl() remains non-null for constructed Schedule.Item instances, as required by both the previous digest implementation and the replacement String.hashCode() call.\n- The supplied target source is empty because this is a new file; production source was not included for direct API verification.\n- This draft assumes the existing testable production API is new Schedule(String scheduleUrl).new Item(String guidOrId), as indicated by the target plan's reference to an existing Schedule/Item setup. Confirm the exact constructor visibility and discriminator semantics during review.\n- The coordinated production edit preserves Schedule.Item.equals(Object) behavior based on the same getUrl()-participating fields, so equal instances constructed with equal schedule URLs and discriminator strings compare equal.\n\n## How was this tested?\n\n- [x] Automated test coverage added in 1 test file(s)\n- [x] Project compiles successfully (`:app:testDebugUnitTest`)\n- [ ] Confirmed by a project maintainer\n\n## Reviewer notes\n\nDo not merge until these assumptions and blockers are resolved:\n\n- The reviewed scope classifies this value as an internal runtime discriminator; no persisted, transmitted, exported, cross-release, or external exact-value consumer was identified.\n- The existing super.hashCode() fallback demonstrates that callers already tolerate non-digest-derived and process-unstable values.\n- getUrl() remains non-null for constructed Schedule.Item instances, as required by both the previous digest implementation and the replacement String.hashCode() call.\n- The supplied target source is empty because this is a new file; production source was not included for direct API verification.\n- This draft assumes the existing testable production API is new Schedule(String scheduleUrl).new Item(String guidOrId), as indicated by the target plan's reference to an existing Schedule/Item setup. Confirm the exact constructor visibility and discriminator semantics during review.\n- The coordinated production edit preserves Schedule.Item.equals(Object) behavior based on the same getUrl()-participating fields, so equal instances constructed with equal schedule URLs and discriminator strings compare equal.\n\n## Additional context\n\nI’m an undergraduate Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB), and this contribution is part of a research project involving software security analysis.\n\nI’m happy to adjust the implementation to better match the project’s architecture, coding conventions, or maintainers’ recommendations.",
